{"profiles":[{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Comb Ducks","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Comb and Knob-billed Ducks","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Sarkidiornis","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Sarkidiornis Eyton, 1838","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2498000},"ottId":88383,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Sarkidiornis","synonyms":["Sarcidiornis"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Sarkidiornis is a genus within the family Anatidae comprising two species that inhabit aquatic environments in tropical and subtropical regions of South America, Africa and southern Asia. Sarkidiornis is sometimes considered a monotypic genus with its sole member the knob-billed duck (S. melanotos), a cosmopolitan species.","revisionId":1345325421,"title":"Sarkidiornis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarkidiornis","wikidataId":"Q2712557"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Nigeria-Cameroon Chimpanzee","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Pan troglodytes ellioti","confidence":98,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Pan troglodytes ellioti (Matschie, 1914)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5786533},"ottId":112831,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Pan troglodytes ellioti","synonyms":["Anthropopithecus ellioti"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzee (Pan troglodytes ellioti) is a subspecies of the common chimpanzee which inhabits the rainforest along the border of Nigeria and Cameroon. Male Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzees can weigh up to 80 kg (180 lb) with a body length of up to 1.2 m (3 ft 11 in) and a height of 1.3 m (4 ft 3 in).","revisionId":1319210020,"title":"Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzee","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nigeria-Cameroon_chimpanzee","wikidataId":"Q3645447"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Alveolate","source":"Wikipedia"}],"ottId":266751,"scientificName":"Alveolata","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The alveolates are a group of protists, considered a major clade within Eukaryota. They are currently grouped with the Stramenopiles and Rhizaria among the protists with tubulocristate mitochondria into the SAR supergroup.","revisionId":1371627958,"title":"Alveolate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alveolate","wikidataId":"Q448746"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Stilts And Avocets","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Stilts","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Avocets","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"stilts & avocets","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Avocets, Stilts","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"},{"language":"en","name":"Stilts, Avocets","source":"IOC World Bird List, v"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Recurvirostridae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Recurvirostridae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4287244},"ottId":703807,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Recurvirostridae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Recurvirostridae are a family of birds in the wader suborder Charadrii. It contains two distinct groups of birds, the avocets (one genus) and the stilts (two genera).","revisionId":1358924645,"title":"Recurvirostridae","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurvirostridae","wikidataId":"Q27042"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Allen's Swamp Monkey","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Swamp Guenon","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Swamp Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Allen s Swamp Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Allen’s Swamp Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Blackish-green Guenon","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Allenopithecus nigroviridis","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Allenopithecus nigroviridis (Pocock, 1907)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436620},"ottId":721471,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Allenopithecus nigroviridis","synonyms":["Cercopithecus nigroviridis"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Allen's swamp monkey (Allenopithecus nigroviridis) is a species of Old World monkey endemic to swamps and riparian forests in the central Congo Basin. As a medium-sized primate, adult individuals can have head-body lengths of up to 18 in (46 cm), with tails being slightly longer.","revisionId":1376191954,"title":"Allen's swamp monkey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allen%27s_swamp_monkey","wikidataId":"Q19486"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Drill","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Mandrill","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Mandrillus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Mandrillus Ritgen, 1824","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5219555},"ottId":741055,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Mandrillus","synonyms":["Chaeropithecus","Drill","Madrillus","Maimon","Mandril","Mandrill","Mormon","Paphio"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Mandrillus is a genus of large Old World monkeys distributed throughout central and southern Africa, consisting of two species: M. sphinx and M. leucophaeus, the mandrill and drill, respectively. Mandrillus, originally placed under the genus Papio as a type of baboon, is closely related to the genus Cercocebus.","revisionId":1369789119,"title":"Mandrillus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandrillus","wikidataId":"Q798426"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Couas","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Coua","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Coua Schinz, 1821","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2496410},"ottId":787071,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Coua","synonyms":["Couas"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Couas are large, mostly terrestrial birds of the cuckoo family, endemic to the island of Madagascar. Couas are reminiscent of African turacos when walking along tree branches, and they likewise feature brightly coloured, bare skin around the eyes.","revisionId":1343009225,"title":"Coua","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coua","wikidataId":"Q869407"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Graells s Tamarin","source":"Mammal Species of the World"},{"language":"en","name":"Graells's Tamarin","source":"Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS)"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Saguinus graellsi","confidence":98,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Saguinus graellsi (Jiménez de la Espada, 1870)","status":"SYNONYM","usageKey":4266887},"ottId":955071,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Saguinus graellsi","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Graells's tamarin, Leontocebus nigricollis graellsi, is a subspecies of the black-mantled tamarin from the northwestern Amazon in southeastern Colombia, eastern Ecuador and northeastern Peru. It differs from other black-mantled tamarins in having a dull olive-brown (no reddish-orange) lower back, rump and thighs.","revisionId":1362943834,"title":"Graells's tamarin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graells%27s_tamarin","wikidataId":"Q21975987"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Maranhão Red-handed Howler Monkey","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Red-handed Howling Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Maranhao Red-handed Howler","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Maranhão Red-handed Howler","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Maranhao Red-handed Howler Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Maranhao Red-handed Howling Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Alouatta ululata","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Alouatta ululata Elliot, 1912","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5786098},"ottId":3607551,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Alouatta ululata","synonyms":["Alouatta belzebul ululata"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Maranhão red-handed howler (Alouatta ululata) is an endangered species of howler monkey endemic to forests (for example Babaçu forests) in the northeastern Brazilian states of Ceará, Maranhão and Piauí. It was previously thought to be a subspecies of the red-handed howler, but unlike that species, the Maranhão red-handed howler is strongly sexually dichromatic.","revisionId":1363965943,"title":"Maranhão red-handed howler","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maranh%C3%A3o_red-handed_howler","wikidataId":"Q1353729"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"False Potto","source":"Mammal Species of the World"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Pseudopotto martini","confidence":98,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Pseudopotto martini Schwartz, 1996","status":"SYNONYM","usageKey":4267327},"ottId":4941439,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Pseudopotto martini","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The false potto (Pseudopotto martini) is a lorisoid primate of uncertain taxonomic status found in Africa. Anthropologist Jeffrey H. Schwartz named it in 1996 as the only species of the genus Pseudopotto on the basis of two specimens (consisting only of skeletal material) that had previously been identified as a potto (Perodicticus).","revisionId":1362852798,"title":"False potto","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_potto","wikidataId":"Q1075190"}},{"commonNames":[],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Notharctus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Notharctus Leidy, 1870","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4827736},"ottId":4941695,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Notharctus","synonyms":["Pelycodus relictus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Notharctus is a genus of adapiform primate that lived in North America during the early to middle Eocene. Known from many geologic formations, including the Bridger Basin and the Devil's Graveyard Formation, Notharctus was wide-ranging throughout North America.","revisionId":1370055877,"title":"Notharctus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notharctus","wikidataId":"Q2086632"}},{"commonNames":